{"name":"Thatch Brown","hex":"#867057","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thatch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Straw, rushes, or similar, used for making or covering the roofs of buildings, or of stacks of hay or grain.","Any of several kinds of palm, the leaves of which are used for thatching."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover the roof with straw, reed, leaves, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thatch"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thatching","blocks":[{"text":"Thatching is the craft of building a roof with dry vegetation such as straw, water reed, sedge, rushes, heather, or palm branches, layering the vegetation so as to shed water away from the inner roof. Since the bulk of the vegetation stays dry and is densely packed—trapping air—thatching also functions as insulation. It is a very old roofing method and has been used in both tropical and temperate climates. Thatch is still employed by builders in developing countries, usually with low-cost local vegetation. By contrast, in some developed countries it is the choice of some affluent people who desire a rustic look for their home, would like a more ecologically friendly roof, or who have purchased an originally thatched abode."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pub.williams.arp.750pix.jpg/330px-Pub.williams.arp.750pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thatching","wikidata":"Q874531"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg/500px-87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg/1280px-87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg/500px-87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inside view of an Inca roof in one of the few reconstructed buildings of Machu Picchu","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A87_-_Machu_Picchu_-_Juin_2009.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/PrayerAtBesakih.jpg/500px-PrayerAtBesakih.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/PrayerAtBesakih.jpg/1280px-PrayerAtBesakih.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/PrayerAtBesakih.jpg/500px-PrayerAtBesakih.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The multi-tiered Meru towers of the Besakih temple in Bali are thatched with black ijuk fibres.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrayerAtBesakih.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Dar%C3%9F.6.JPG/250px-Dar%C3%9F.6.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org